<h2>Who clear aligners work for</h2>
<p>Aligners are excellent for the most common adult cases: mild to moderate crowding, gaps, post-braces relapse, and rotated teeth. For severe bite issues or complex skeletal problems, traditional braces or an orthodontic referral may be a better fit — and we'll tell you up front if that's the case.</p>

<h2>How treatment works</h2>
<ol class="checklist" style="list-style:decimal;padding-left:28px">
  <li><strong>Digital scan.</strong> No goopy impressions — a quick digital scan of your teeth.</li>
  <li><strong>Treatment plan.</strong> You see a 3D simulation of how your teeth will move over time.</li>
  <li><strong>Aligners.</strong> Each clear tray moves your teeth a small, calibrated amount. You switch trays every 1–2 weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Refinement.</strong> Most cases finish with a short refinement series to lock in the result.</li>
  <li><strong>Retainers.</strong> Lifetime nighttime retainer wear keeps your new alignment stable. This part is non-negotiable.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Combining with whitening or veneers</h2>
<p>Many patients align first, then whiten or place veneers — straight teeth take less restorative work and look better when finished. We'll lay out the right sequence at your consultation.</p>
